+- 2026-11-14 (3): `Nibbles` (block metadata) is an `Arc` (`mc-rs/src/world/chunk.rs`): `Option<Arc<Vec<u8>>>`, `None` = all 0; a clone shares the array, a real write goes through `Arc::make_mut` (copies once while a mesh job holds it), writing the value already there copies nothing. Why: `MeshJob`'s five `Snap`s still cloned `data` on the render thread, 5 x 48 KB wherever tall grass grows (`VOLUME / 2` = 48 KB since H = 384). Item 2 of "Memory and speed" (`Arc` for `blocks` / `light`) was already in the code (`Entry`, `Snap`, `LChunk`) and the list above is stale on that point; item 3 (light deltas) is NOT worth doing: `Report.chunks` carries an `Arc::clone`, not a copy, and the only copy is `Arc::make_mut` in `starlight` on the light thread, once per chunk per report; a delta would make the render thread patch its array, i.e. copy it there. Tests: the new `Nibbles` behaviour (clone shares, write copies once, same-value write copies nothing, zero equals none) passed standalone with rustc 1.75; the crate was not built (no wgpu / Android toolchain in the session), so `cargo test -p mc-rs` is still to run.
 - 2026-11-14: a GPU clock, upload staging and a draw count on the OpenGL ES renderer (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not measured; +1 test `gles::timer::tests::a_result_counts_only_when_it_is_whole_and_believable`). Why: the GLES path had no GPU time at all (`Backend::gpu_ms` returned nothing for it), so the dynamic resolution judged the frame by its swap wait, which vsync pads and a panel above 60 Hz turns into "always slow", and sank to `SCALE_MIN` whatever the cost was made of. A debug-screen shot (Adreno 610, scene 802 x 360 at 50%, CPU 5 ms, 55 fps, p99 and worst 21 ms) shows a frame whose pixel count cannot be what limits it: a quarter of the pixels of the 100% scene and still 18 ms, so what is left is per-triangle work, a wait that is not the GPU's, or both. 1. `gles/timer.rs` `GpuTimer`: `GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query`, one `TIME_ELAPSED_EXT` query around the scene pass (the final pass and the HUD are not in it, as on the wgpu path), six slots, a result is read only when `QUERY_RESULT_AVAILABLE` says it is there (never waits) and dropped when the disjoint flag is set. `Renderer::gpu_ms` -> `Backend::gpu_ms` -> `Drs::tick` (the GPU-time path that was already written for wgpu: down above 85% of the panel's frame time, probe up under 70%) and the `gpu` figure of the debug screen's Frame row; the pacing log line prints it too. A context without the extension keeps the old behaviour. 2. `Gl::write`: a write into a buffer of 256 KiB or more (the 12 MiB chunk pages) goes through a staging buffer and `glCopyBufferSubData` instead of `glBufferSubData`: the copy is a command in the GPU's own queue, so the driver neither waits for the draws still queued on that page nor clones it. UNVERIFIED that the Adreno 610 driver did either: compare `p99` and `worst` while flying, before and after. `Buf` carries its `size` now. `tools/gles_timer_check.py` runs the staged write and the query ring on Mesa (GLES 3.2, llvmpipe): the 12 MiB buffer comes out byte for byte as with direct writes and 39 of 40 frames are timed with no GL error; that checks the GL calls and constants only, not the Rust and not the speed. 3. Debug screen row `Drawn` (GLES only): chunk draw calls and triangles of the last frame (`Renderer::drawn`, counted in `runs` and `flat_runs`). **To read on a device:** `gpu` against the frame time. `gpu` close to the frame time: GPU-bound; then a `gpu` that did not fall when the scale did (compare 50% with 70%) is per-triangle work, and `Drawn` gives the triangle count to divide it by. `gpu` well under the frame time: the wait is elsewhere (a vsync step, the `Perf` refresh hint, the compositor), and lowering the scene does nothing. **Consequence to know:** the scale can now rise above the floor it used to sit on, because it follows the scene pass's own time; the full-size final pass is outside that figure, so if frames get longer after this change, raise `GPU_DOWN`'s margin (the share left for the final pass) before anything else.
 - 2026-11-14 (2): `Performance: Steady 60` (`fpsLimit:3`) (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run on a device). Why: on a 90 Hz panel a frame can only be shown every 11.1 or 22.2 ms, so a game at 55-60 fps alternates 90 and 45 fps (the 21 ms worst frame on the Adreno 610 shot), and `Drs` chasing 90 fps keeps the scene near `SCALE_MIN`. What: a 16.7 ms frame cap, `ANativeWindow_setFrameRate(60)` (the compositor may switch the panel to 60 Hz), and `Drs` aims at `min(panel Hz, cap fps)` every frame, so it holds the scene to 60 fps instead of 90 and the scale can rise. Default stays Balanced. **To read on a device:** `p99` and `worst` near 17 ms and a higher `Dyn. res` percent than at Balanced; if the hint is ignored (log `frame rate hint 60 Hz -> N`, N != 0) set the panel to 60 Hz in Android settings.
 - 2026-11-08 (6): Cascades only: the corner columns of the density are kept between chunks (`tectonic/src/lib.rs`; written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not measured; +1 test `tectonic::tests::the_corner_cache_changes_no_density`). `Tectonic::chunk` evaluates the density tree on the 5 x 5 corner columns of a chunk's 4 x 4 cells (pass 1, `interps.len()` tables x 49 levels each, the 3D noise of the caves and the terrain: nearly all of the tree's nodes are under an `interpolated` node, pass 2 reads the tables). The 16 corner columns on a chunk's edge are its neighbours' too, and every chunk made them again (the `ponytail` note of 2026-11-05: ~56 % more 2D work than needed). Now `Tectonic::cascades` carries `corners`: the edge columns, by block (x, z), `interps.len() * ny` f64 each, in a FIFO of `CORNER_CAP` 1536 (about 5 MB) that every `Tectonic::cascades` of one seed shares (`shared_corners`; each worker makes its own `Tectonic`, so a chunk next to one another worker made still hits), told apart by `layout`, a fingerprint of the tables' values at a few points, so two bundles whose tables differ never share one. A chunk takes the edge columns that exist and evaluates the other 9 (the inside ones, never shared) and the missing edge ones: at best 9 of 25 columns, at the worst (the first chunk, or a far jump) all 25, about a third less of pass 1 while walking. A hit is the f64 the loop would have made (the memo only caches a pure function), so the density is bit for bit the same: the test compares a cached terrain with an uncached one on three neighbouring chunks and on a repeat. Tectonic's and Beta's worlds are not touched (`corners` is `None` there). UNVERIFIED: that it compiles, that the test passes, and the gain on a device (a first walk into new ground is where it shows: read the debug screen's Frame row). Not done: the aquifers (`tectonic::aquifer::fluids`) were not read for the same kind of repeat, and pass 2 still evaluates the remaining tree on all 98 304 blocks.
